Day 1 — Writing and interpreting filtered SQL queries
The basic structure
SELECT "column 1", "column 2"
FROM vax
WHERE "some column" = 'some value'
ORDER BY "column 1" ASC;
SELECT identifies columns to return.
FROM vax identifies the table.
WHERE defines conditions for matching rows.
AND requires all specified conditions to be true.
ORDER BY sorts the selected rows; ASC is ascending and DESC is descending.
DISTINCT removes duplicate combinations of selected columns, not necessarily duplicate records throughout the dataset.
Double quotes identify SQLite column names containing spaces or special characters; single quotes delimit text values.
Practice query 1: Perceived equity in seeking healthcare
Question: How do estimates of receiving a 2024–2025 COVID-19 dose compare across self-perceived healthcare-equity categories among adults nationally?

SELECT DISTINCT
    "Group Category",
    "Estimate (%)",
    "95% CI (%)",
    "Sample Size"
FROM vax
WHERE "Geography Type" = 'National'
  AND "Vaccine" = 'COVID-19'
  AND "Group Name" = 'Self-perceived equity in seeking healthcare compared to other races/ethnicities'
  AND "Indicator Category" LIKE 'Received a 2024-2025 COVID-19 dose%'
  AND "Time Period" LIKE 'April%'
ORDER BY CAST("Estimate (%)" AS REAL) DESC;
How it works: Group Name selects the equity topic; Group Category displays the possible responses. The remaining conditions hold the geography, vaccine, outcome, and time period constant so the percentages can be compared. LIKE 'April%' matches text beginning with April. The CAST(... AS REAL) makes numeric ordering explicit if the imported percentage field is text. Before using the cast, confirm that the field contains numeric percentages, not strings with % symbols or suppression markers.

Results reported in the prior walkthrough (verify against local output):

Self-perceived equity category	Estimate (%)	Reported 95% CI (%)	Sample size
Better than other races or ethnicities	35.2	32.5–37.8	8,327
Don't know / refused	18.9	17.0–20.9	7,020
Same as other races or ethnicities	16.5	15.3–17.6	19,333
Worse than other races or ethnicities	13.2	8.5–18.0	1,108
Interpretation: The reported percentages vary by perceived access equity. This is an association in survey data—not evidence that perceptions caused vaccination behavior. Verify that each selected row represents the same indicator and reporting period before using these numbers.

Practice query 2: Race/ethnicity comparison
Question: Which adult race/ethnicity categories have higher and lower estimated coverage in the April reporting period?

SELECT DISTINCT
    "Group Category" AS race_ethnicity,
    "Estimate (%)",
    "95% CI (%)",
    "Sample Size"
FROM vax
WHERE "Geography Type" = 'National'
  AND "Vaccine" = 'COVID-19'
  AND TRIM("Group Name") = 'Race/Ethnicity (7 level)'
  AND "Indicator Category" LIKE 'Received a 2024-2025 COVID-19 dose%'
  AND "Time Period" LIKE 'April%'
ORDER BY CAST("Estimate (%)" AS REAL) DESC;
Here, AS race_ethnicity gives Group Category a useful output name; it does not change the actual database. TRIM strips leading/trailing spaces from a text value.

Results discussed previously (verify before publication as findings):

White, non-Hispanic adults: 23.4% (reported as the highest estimate in that comparison).
American Indian/Alaska Native adults: 7.6% (95% CI 4.6–10.6; n = 364).
Native Hawaiian/Pacific Islander adults: 9.4% (95% CI 5.3–13.6; n = 177).
Careful finding: The earlier query discussion described a range from 23.4% among White non-Hispanic adults to 7.6% among American Indian/Alaska Native adults. The estimates for some smaller groups had wide, overlapping confidence intervals and limited samples; the ranking of their point estimates should not be interpreted as a statistically established difference.

What I learned: A point estimate is not the whole story. Confidence intervals express estimation uncertainty. Overlapping intervals do not prove the absence of a difference, and a formal test of differences would need to account for the survey design and how estimates were produced.

Day 2 — IN, BETWEEN, LIKE, DISTINCT, ORDER BY, and LIMIT
SQLBolt-style quick reference
Concept	Example	Meaning
IN	WHERE "Vaccine" IN ('COVID-19', 'Flu')	Matches any item in a specified list
BETWEEN	WHERE "Sample Size" BETWEEN 100 AND 500	Inclusive lower and upper bounds (when the field is numeric)
LIKE	WHERE "Time Period" LIKE 'April%'	Matches a text pattern; % means zero or more characters
DISTINCT	SELECT DISTINCT "Group Name"	Returns each selected value/combination only once
ORDER BY	ORDER BY "Sample Size" DESC	Sorts from largest to smallest
LIMIT	LIMIT 10	Returns at most 10 rows after other conditions are applied
TRIM	TRIM("Group Name") = 'Sex'	Ignores spaces at the start/end of the text
AS	"Group Category" AS Sex	Renames a field in the displayed results
BETWEEN compares according to the stored column type. Don't assume a text date range such as April 1 - April 27 can be treated as a real date, or that a text percentage will sort numerically.

Practice query 3: Compare December with April, by age
Question: How did Georgia age-group estimates for receipt of a 2024–2025 COVID-19 dose differ between the December and April reporting periods?

First, get the exact reporting labels; don't guess them:

SELECT DISTINCT "Year", "Time Period"
FROM vax
WHERE "Time Period" LIKE 'December%'
   OR "Time Period" LIKE 'April%'
ORDER BY "Year", "Time Period";
Then replace the placeholder labels below with those you see in your database:

SELECT DISTINCT
    "Year",
    "Time Period",
    "Group Category" AS age_group,
    "Indicator Category",
    "Estimate (%)",
    "95% CI (%)",
    "Sample Size"
FROM vax
WHERE "Geography" = 'Georgia'
  AND "Vaccine" = 'COVID-19'
  AND TRIM("Group Name") = 'Age'
  AND "Indicator Category" LIKE 'Received a 2024-2025 COVID-19 dose%'
  AND "Time Period" IN ('EXACT DECEMBER LABEL', 'EXACT APRIL LABEL')
ORDER BY age_group, "Year", "Time Period";
How it works: IN replaces a long OR condition on the same column; the ORDER BY groups matching age categories together. The outcome filter is important: without it, the query might mix vaccinated, unvaccinated, confidence, and access questions. Report the year with the label because a reporting-period string may span two calendar years.

